  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAfternoon conditions across the forecast area indicate fairly light \nto modest winds and mild temperatures for late September. Readings \nhave climbed into the mid 70s to low 80s underneath mostly sunny to \npartly cloudy skies. The northern half of the CWA remains sandwiched \nin between a couple areas of cloud cover. To the south, thickening \nhigh level cloudiness is streaming northward across southern parts \nof the state into portions of south central and east central SD. \nThis is associated with a low pressure system that will pull \nnortheast into the Central Plains tonight. Scattered low to mid \nlevel clouds are drifting east and southeast out of MT into ND this \nafternoon in response to a sfc cold front that is moving southeast.\n\nThis cold front is progged to slip south and east through the CWA \nlate tonight into early Thursday. Mainly dry conditions are expected \nas winds turn more north to northwesterly overnight. A few CAM \nsolutions want to generate some light shower activity on either side \nof the midnight hour tonight across the ND/SD border area. BUFKIT \nsoundings look meager for precipitation so went with a dry forecast \nfor now. But, something to keep an eye on for later shifts. A cold \nair advection patter sets up behind the fropa. 850mb temps drop from \nthe teens into the single digits C between this evening and daybreak \nThursday. A much cooler air mass will be in place on Thursday with \ndaytime readings only expected to reach the upper 50s to mid 60s. \nSome sunshine is expected but if any cloud cover persists long \nenough through the day, those locales could remain cooler than that. \nWith the center of a sfc ridge still off to our northwest during the \nday, a breezy northerly wind may make it feel even cooler. This high \npressure system will track across the area Thursday night leading to \na cool overnight with readings in the 40s and perhaps a few upper \n30s along the ND/SD border. A weak mid level disturbance and upper \njet streak traversing through parts of western/central SD along with \na little mid level warm air advection may be enough to kick off some \nlight rain showers across mainly central SD Thursday night into \nFriday morning. Rainfall amounts at this point look to be spotty and \nmeager.\n\nA warm air advection pattern returns toward the end of the week as \nthe sfc high slips farther away from the region to the south and \neast and the next disturbance approaches from the northwest. The \nwarmer air mass doesn't quite make it into the area in time on \nFriday so anticipate temperatures to remain on the cooler side of \nnormal. A sfc low and associated cold front will move through the \nDakotas on Saturday. Mainly dry conditions are now expected locally \nwith the bulk of the moisture remaining to our north and east. Temps \nmay rebound closer to normal Saturday, depending on the timing of \nthe fropa. Otherwise, we'll see cooler conditions prevail through \nthe end of the weekend. Temperatures are then progged to rebound \ncloser to normal if not above normal the first half of next week as \nanother sfc high pressure system shifts east of our region and the \nnext upper level trough and associated cold front works across \nsouthern Canada by late in the forecast period.",
